.TH MYGREP 3 "November 2026" "libmyutils 0.4.1" "libmyutils Manual"
.SH NAME
mygrep \- search a file for lines matching a pattern
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <myfilefunctions.h>
.sp
.BI "int mygrep(const char *" filename ", const char *" pattern );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B mygrep
function opens the file named by
.I filename
and scans it line by line for the literal substring
.IR pattern ,
using
.BR strstr (3).
Each matching line is printed to
.I stdout
in the form
.IR filename : line_number : line_text ,
similar to
.BR grep (1).
.PP
Note that
.I pattern
is matched as a literal substring, not a regular expression.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns the number of matching lines found (which may be 0).
Returns \-1 if
.I filename
or
.I pattern
is
.B NULL
, or the file could not be opened.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
#include <myfilefunctions.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int matches = mygrep("notes.txt", "TODO");
    printf("%d matches\en", matches);
    return 0;
}
.fi
.SH AUTHOR
Written as part of the OS Assignment 01: Core C Utilities Library.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR grep (1),
.BR strstr (3)
